# external-services.yaml — services this installation provides OUTSIDE the cluster
#
# Empty by default, and an empty file changes nothing: every service deploys
# in-cluster exactly as it always has. A developer on Rancher Desktop never needs
# to touch this file.
#
# Declare a service here when this installation already runs it elsewhere — a
# database on another host, an object store on the hypervisor. `uis deploy <id>`
# then deploys a transparent proxy instead of the real thing: same Service name,
# same labels, same in-cluster address. Nothing downstream changes, because
# nothing downstream can tell the difference.
#
# ⚠️ ADDRESSES ONLY, NEVER CREDENTIALS. Secrets belong in `urbalurba-secrets`.
#
# ⚠️ Prefer a private/backplane address over a tunnelled one when the service and
# the cluster sit on the same host. Routing that traffic over a VPN makes an
# in-house dependency rely on an external coordination service being reachable.
#
# Format:
#
#   <service-id>:
#     host: <address>        # reachable from inside the cluster
#     port: <number>         # optional; defaults to the service's normal port
#     why:  <text>           # REQUIRED - see below
#
# `why:` is required and the deploy fails without it. An external dependency
# nobody can justify is one nobody maintains, and when it breaks at 3am the first
# question is why anything was pointed there in the first place.
#
# Example — the reference installation's PostgreSQL, which runs in a container on
# the hypervisor rather than in the cluster:
#
#   postgresql:
#     host: 10.10.0.105
#     port: 5432
#     why: "production database on Odin CT 105, reached over the vmbr1 backplane"
#
# To go back to running it in-cluster, delete the entry and redeploy.
